Three agent definitions in this directory are adapted from the `agent-compatibility`
plugin in github.com/cursor/plugins, MIT-licensed, at commit 461255613064:

  docs-reliability-review.md   <- agent-compatibility/agents/docs-reliability-review.md
  startup-review.md            <- agent-compatibility/agents/startup-review.md
  validation-review.md         <- agent-compatibility/agents/validation-review.md

What was taken: the review dimensions, the scoring anchors, the instruction to pick a
specific score rather than a round bucket, the "score the damage from the drift rather
than the existence of drift" rule, and the guardrail against inferring a startup failure
from a bound port or an existing process.

What was changed: the upstream workflow opens by running a published Cursor CLI scanner
and blending its deterministic score at 70%. That CLI is not installed here and would be
a third-party dependency for a review that does not need one, so step one is dropped and
each agent reads this repository's own surfaces instead. Every agent is grounded in
measured local instances -- the ratchet with no caller, the false light-block claim, the
zombie socket, the vacuously-green assertions -- because a rubric that names the defect
a repository has actually paid for is worth more than a generic one.

Frontmatter is translated to the Claude Code shape: `readonly: true` becomes a tool list
with no Edit or Write, and `model: fast` becomes an explicit model.

The twenty SaaS-integration plugins in that marketplace are deliberately not adopted.
This repository runs Postgres behind its own read-only API; a Salesforce or HubSpot
connector is context spent on a stack that is not here, which is the cost the console's
own plugin trim of 2026-08-21 measured at roughly 15,000 tokens per session.

